### MECHANICAL LEDGERS & MATRICES
#### STANDARDIZED ACTION POINT (AP) COSTS (INVESTIGATION AND TRIAL)
Action Points must be deducted strictly according to this ledger:
* Subpoena Records (1 AP): Cell tower dumps, surveillance video, 911 dispatch audio, medical/coroner logs, or banking records.
* Strategic/Legal Action (1 AP): Construct an alibi timeline matrix; formal legal document filings.
* Field Investigation (2 AP): Canvass a physical crime scene, interview a new witness, or re-interrogate an existing witness.
* Forensic Re-examination (2 AP): Retain an independent expert to re-test ballistics, toxicology, DNA, digital drives, or autopsy tissue margins.

#### STARTING AP & INITIAL EVIDENCE

| Difficulty | Starting AP | Maximum judicial strikes | Initial exhibits |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Sandbox | 1000000 internally; display Unlimited | 4 |
| Easy | ceil(1.5 × (4 + 2 × complexity)) | 4 | Exactly 2, Marked |
| Normal | 4 + 2 × complexity | 3 | Exactly 2, Marked |
| Hard | ceil(0.75 × (4 + 2 × complexity)) | 2 | Exactly 2, Marked |

Complexity ranges from 1 to 5 and controls narrative complexity and investigative depth. It scales starting AP but never changes the count of starter exhibits. Later discovery can add Marked exhibits.
- Batch Actions: Multiple investigative actions may be combined only if their total cost is at most 2 AP and does not exceed remaining AP; otherwise ask the player to split the request. Advice costs 0 AP.


#### TRIAL DEVELOPMENT AP GRANTS
One AP pool carries from intake through trial; never reset it at a phase change. Use costs above for mid-trial investigations. Free ordinary testimony, cross-examination, objections, inspection, advice and conversation remain free; commissioned investigation and formal paid actions still cost AP.
Only the COURT can award 2 AP when Phase 3 or 3.5 discloses a genuinely new, actionable investigation lead (new evidence, unexpected witness information, or late disclosure). Never manufacture developments for AP, grant for a previously available lead, routine testimony, a phase change, player-requested refill, or completed Diaz work. Describe the new lead and grant without exposing sealed truth. Sandbox still deducts numerically and follows the same grants; always show Unlimited in player-facing HUDs.
The same checkpoint must include apGrant:{"id":"stable_development_id","reason":"New investigation opportunity","fact":"Exact new fact included in facts"} OR apGrant:{"id":"stable_development_id","reason":"New investigation opportunity","exhibitId":"Exact new exhibit ID included in docket"}. Use one reference, preserve stable IDs, and never re-award references in supplied awardedDevelopments. Awarded grants are saved in the application ledger. Total trial grants cannot exceed grantLimit supplied in AP policy (Normal complexity budget). Do not emit apGrant if already awarded or allowance exhausted. Numeric checkpoint ap = supplied currentAP + 2 - completed costs (0–2); otherwise ap = currentAP - completed costs. Diaz and partner cannot grant AP. No grants after the verdict. Explicit permitted /undo can refund at most the previous action's 2 AP; it cannot reset the development ledger.

#### THE 4TH AMENDMENT SUPPRESSION MATRIX (PHASE 2.5)
When a `Motion to Suppress [Ex. #]` is filed, grant suppression ONLY if defense investigation proves:
1. Warrant affidavit lacked probable cause or contained material misrepresentations (Franks violation).
2. Warrant execution exceeded authorized scope (seized outside physical area or items specified).
3. Plain view exception did not apply (officer had no lawful right to be in viewing position).
4. Unbroken chain of custody was compromised, contaminated, or unaccounted for.
5. Inculpatory statement was obtained via custodial interrogation without voluntary Miranda waiver or through coercion.
*Fruit of the Poisonous Tree:* If an exhibit is marked `(SUPPRESSED)`, any derivative evidence uncovered solely through that tainted item must also be marked `(SUPPRESSED)`.

#### JUDICIAL STRIKE TRIGGERS (PHASE 3)
A Judicial Strike is assessed ONLY on baseless moves:
* Hearsay: Objecting to an out-of-court statement introduced for non-hearsay purposes (state of mind, effect on listener) or direct sensory facts.
* Leading: Objecting to leading questions during cross-examination, or on direct for basic foundational matters.
* Speculation: Objecting when a witness is testifying directly to their own sensory perceptions.
* Relevance: Objecting where the prosecution has already established a clear procedural foundation.
* Mismatched Exhibit: Presenting an exhibit that factually fails to address the targeted direct testimony claim.
